q('A 67-year-old has recurrent unprovoked backward falls within two years of onset, axial rigidity and slow vertical saccades. Which diagnosis best fits?', 'Progressive supranuclear palsy, Richardson phenotype',[
 ('Typical Parkinson disease','Early repeated falls and vertical ocular motor dysfunction are important atypical features.'),('Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar phenotype','Cerebellar and autonomic findings would be more characteristic than this ocular-postural combination.'),('Dementia with Lewy bodies','Early cognitive fluctuations and visual hallucinations are not the defining features presented.')],
 'Recognize a postural and ocular motor pattern', '**Early falls with vertical saccadic dysfunction strongly suggest PSP.**', ['Axial dysfunction may dominate over limb tremor.','Different PSP phenotypes can initially lack the full Richardson pattern.'], '**Examine eye movements rather than relying on gait alone.**', ['Document saccadic speed, range and postural responses.','Swallowing, falls prevention and communication require early attention.'], [['PSP','Early falls and vertical eye signs'],['MSA','Autonomic/cerebellar syndrome'],['PD','Often sustained levodopa-responsive limb syndrome']], [PSP], 'clinical_scenario')
q('In early vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, which maneuver may partly overcome the voluntary gaze limitation?', 'Vestibulo-ocular reflex testing with appropriate safety precautions',[
 ('Testing accommodation alone','Near response does not demonstrate the vestibular bypass of supranuclear control.'),('Repeating the same voluntary saccade command','This still depends on the impaired supranuclear pathway.'),('Testing the corneal reflex','This tests a different brainstem reflex arc.')],
 'Supranuclear and nuclear limitations differ', '**Preserved reflex movement despite impaired voluntary gaze supports a supranuclear lesion.**', ['The reflex may become impaired later.','Do not perform unsafe head movements in cervical disease or trauma.'], '**Interpret the maneuver alongside saccadic slowing.**', ['Restriction from orbital or muscle disease has a different mechanism.','A single ocular sign does not establish the full pathological diagnosis.'], [['Voluntary gaze','May be restricted'],['Vestibular response','May remain relatively preserved early'],['Late disease','Reflex limitation can develop']], [PSP])
q('Parkinsonism is accompanied early by urinary retention, neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, cerebellar ataxia and nocturnal stridor. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Multiple system atrophy',[
 ('Typical tremor-dominant Parkinson disease','Severe early autonomic, cerebellar and laryngeal findings are atypical.'),('Progressive supranuclear palsy','Vertical gaze palsy and early falls are more defining clues.'),('Corticobasal syndrome','Asymmetric cortical motor and sensory signs are expected.')],
 'Combine autonomic failure with the motor syndrome', '**MSA can combine parkinsonian, cerebellar and autonomic dysfunction.**', ['Retention warrants assessment of postvoid residual and other causes.','Stridor can threaten breathing and needs specialist evaluation.'], '**Distinguish diagnostic certainty from suspicion.**', ['Apply the full MDS criteria and exclusions.','Some patients obtain a partial levodopa response.'], [['MSA-P','Parkinsonian predominance'],['MSA-C','Cerebellar predominance'],['Autonomic failure','Central diagnostic component']], [MSA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A hot-cross-bun pontine MRI sign is found in a patient with progressive ataxia. What is the best interpretation?', 'It supports an appropriate MSA-C phenotype but is not specific enough to establish MSA by itself',[
 ('It proves MSA regardless of clinical findings','Similar appearances occur in other cerebellar degenerations.'),('It establishes idiopathic Parkinson disease','It is not a diagnostic marker of typical PD.'),('It excludes a degenerative cerebellar disorder','It can be a degenerative imaging clue.')],
 'Imaging adds support rather than replacing clinical criteria', '**The pontine pattern reflects degeneration of selected transverse fibers.**', ['Clinical context and alternative causes remain essential.','MRI markers contribute to clinically established MSA criteria.'], '**Do not diagnose a syndrome from a memorable image alone.**', ['Review autonomic and motor findings.','Investigate hereditary or acquired ataxias where indicated.'], [['Pontine sign','Supportive in context'],['Specificity','Limited'],['Diagnosis','Integrated clinical and imaging assessment']], [MSA])
q('Dementia, recurrent formed visual hallucinations and cognitive fluctuations begin six months before spontaneous parkinsonism. Which label best follows the clinical timing convention?', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies',[
 ('Parkinson disease dementia','That convention is used when dementia develops after well-established PD, beyond the one-year boundary.'),('Pure Alzheimer disease without further assessment','The fluctuation-hallucination-parkinsonism combination points toward Lewy body disease.'),('Multiple system atrophy','Prominent early dementia and hallucinations argue against that attribution.')],
 'Use the one-year convention with its limitations', '**Dementia preceding or arising within one year of parkinsonism is conventionally termed DLB.**', ['The timing boundary is pragmatic rather than a distinct biological divide.','REM sleep behavior disorder is another core clinical feature.'], '**Medication sensitivity changes management.**', ['Potent dopamine blockade can provoke severe reactions.','Assess delirium and other contributors to fluctuation.'], [['DLB','Dementia before/near motor onset'],['PD dementia','Dementia after established PD'],['Biology','Substantial overlap']], [DLB], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has markedly asymmetric rigidity, limb apraxia, cortical sensory loss and an alien-limb phenomenon. What is the most accurate clinical conclusion?', 'Corticobasal syndrome, with the underlying pathology still requiring assessment',[
 ('Pathologically proven corticobasal degeneration','The syndrome can result from more than one pathology.'),('Pure peripheral neuropathy','Apraxia and cortical sensory dysfunction cannot be explained by a peripheral lesion alone.'),('Typical essential tremor','This does not account for the cortical and akinetic-rigid signs.')],
 'Separate the clinical syndrome from histopathology', '**CBS combines asymmetric motor dysfunction with higher cortical signs.**', ['Corticobasal degeneration is one possible substrate.','Alzheimer and other pathologies can produce a similar phenotype.'], '**Describe what the examination establishes.**', ['Assess language, cognition and asymmetric cortical dysfunction.','Use biomarkers where they meaningfully refine the etiological differential.'], [['CBS','Clinical phenotype'],['CBD','Specific pathology'],['Apraxia','Impaired learned action beyond weakness']], [CBS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Symmetric rigidity and bradykinesia begin after several months of metoclopramide. Which initial strategy is most appropriate?', 'Review and withdraw or replace the dopamine-blocking drug when feasible, then reassess',[
 ('Diagnose idiopathic PD solely from the exposure history','The temporal drug association makes drug-induced parkinsonism an important alternative.'),('Increase metoclopramide to reverse the rigidity','Additional dopamine blockade can worsen the syndrome.'),('Use the symmetry to exclude all degenerative disease','Drug exposure can unmask underlying PD; symmetry is not decisive.')],
 'Medication history is part of the neurological examination', '**D2 blockade can create a parkinsonian syndrome.**', ['Antipsychotics and certain antiemetics are common contributors.','Pure drug-induced disease may have preserved presynaptic transporter imaging.'], '**Reassess after a supervised medication change.**', ['Recovery may take time.','Persistent or progressive findings warrant evaluation for underlying degeneration.'], [['D2 blocker','Potential cause'],['Normal DaT imaging','Compatible with pure drug effect'],['Persistence','Consider unmasked disease']], [NI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An older adult has gait initiation failure, urinary urgency, cognitive slowing and ventriculomegaly. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic principle?', 'Evaluate for iNPH, including the imaging pattern and objective gait response to CSF drainage when appropriate',[
 ('Diagnose iNPH from ventriculomegaly alone','Atrophy and other causes can enlarge the ventricles.'),('Exclude iNPH because the full triad is not equally severe','Gait can predominate and the manifestations need not be equally advanced.'),('Guarantee shunt success after any subjective improvement','Selection and objective assessment matter; improvement is not a guarantee.')],
 'Potential reversibility requires careful selection', '**A characteristic gait syndrome and imaging distribution increase suspicion for iNPH.**', ['DESH can support the diagnosis.','Use standardized walking measures before and after drainage.'], '**A negative tap test does not invariably exclude shunt responsiveness.**', ['Consider timing, sensitivity, comorbidity and further specialist assessment.','Counsel about benefits and surgical risks.'], [['Ventriculomegaly','Necessary context, not sufficient alone'],['Tap response','Supports potential benefit'],['Negative test','Does not absolutely exclude']], [NPH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Predominant lower-body gait difficulty, pyramidal signs and extensive vascular lesions raise concern for vascular parkinsonism. Which caution is most appropriate?', 'White-matter lesions alone do not prove that vascular disease causes the motor syndrome',[
 ('Any age-related MRI lesion establishes the diagnosis','Incidental vascular changes are frequent.'),('Levodopa responsiveness absolutely excludes a vascular contribution','Some patients, especially with nigrostriatal involvement, can respond.'),('A normal arm examination proves idiopathic PD','The distribution and overall syndrome require interpretation.')],
 'Require a plausible clinical and anatomical relationship', '**Lower-body gait dysfunction has several competing causes.**', ['Consider iNPH, frontal degeneration, neuropathy and orthopedic limitation.','Vascular and neurodegenerative disease can coexist.'], '**Treat vascular risk while refining the motor diagnosis.**', ['Use a supervised symptomatic treatment trial where appropriate.','Avoid assigning causality from imaging burden alone.'], [['MRI vascular burden','Possible contributor'],['Temporal/anatomical relation','Strengthens causality'],['Mixed disease','Common diagnostic possibility']], [NI,NPH])
q('Which presentation best matches the MDS essential tremor syndrome definition?', 'Isolated bilateral upper-limb action tremor for at least three years without definite dystonia, ataxia or parkinsonism',[
 ('Unilateral rest tremor with definite bradykinesia','This supports a parkinsonian syndrome.'),('Isolated head tremor with a persistent abnormal neck posture','Dystonic tremor is an important consideration.'),('New bilateral action tremor for three weeks','Duration and reversible causes require assessment before applying the defined ET syndrome.')],
 'Define the tremor syndrome before treating it', '**ET is an action-tremor syndrome with a specified duration and absence of other definite neurological signs.**', ['Head or voice tremor may coexist with the qualifying upper-limb tremor.','Family history or alcohol responsiveness is not mandatory.'], '**Look for medication and metabolic contributors.**', ['Thyroid disease and enhanced physiological tremor can mimic ET.','An evolving syndrome requires reassessment.'], [['ET','Bilateral upper-limb action tremor'],['Duration','At least three years'],['Other definite signs','Suggest another or combined syndrome']], [TR])
q('Disabling essential hand tremor requires medication, but the patient has active bronchial asthma. Which option is most reasonable?', 'Consider low-dose primidone with cautious titration rather than nonselective propranolol',[
 ('Prefer propranolol because asthma protects against bronchospasm','Nonselective beta blockade can provoke bronchospasm.'),('Use levodopa as the usual first-line ET drug','It is used for parkinsonism rather than routine ET.'),('Start primidone at a high maintenance dose immediately','Early sedation, nausea and ataxia can limit tolerability.')],
 'Match symptomatic therapy to comorbidity', '**Propranolol and primidone are established options for limb ET.**', ['Contraindications can determine the choice.','Primidone requires slow introduction, especially in older patients.'], '**Measure functional benefit as well as tremor amplitude.**', ['Review writing, eating and adverse effects.','Other drug or procedural options may be considered for persistent disability.'], [['Propranolol','Avoid with relevant bronchospastic disease'],['Primidone','Titrate cautiously'],['Outcome','Function and tolerability']], [ET], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An irregular head tremor improves at a particular head angle and with lightly touching the chin. Which diagnosis is favored?', 'Dystonic tremor associated with cervical dystonia',[
 ('Essential tremor solely because the head is involved','Isolated head tremor with dystonic signs requires a different classification.'),('Parkinsonian rest tremor','The posture dependence and sensory trick favor dystonia.'),('Cerebellar intention tremor','That typically increases near a target and is accompanied by cerebellar findings.')],
 'A null point and sensory trick are useful clues', '**Dystonic tremor is often irregular and linked to abnormal posture.**', ['A geste antagoniste can transiently improve the posture or tremor.','Subtle dystonia can be missed without observing several tasks.'], '**Classify the movement before selecting therapy.**', ['Targeted treatment of the dystonic muscles may help.','A sensory trick does not imply a voluntary or fabricated symptom.'], [['Null point','Position with reduced tremor'],['Sensory trick','Supports dystonic phenomenology'],['Posture','Examine for dystonia']], [TR,DY], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Legs feel unsteady only while standing still; walking or sitting relieves the symptom. Surface EMG shows a coherent 15-Hz leg tremor. Which diagnosis fits best?', 'Primary orthostatic tremor',[
 ('Orthostatic hypotension','The electrophysiological tremor and relief pattern are not explained by BP alone.'),('Classic slow parkinsonian rest tremor','The activation condition and high frequency differ.'),('Cerebellar intention tremor','This is a target-directed movement phenomenon.')],
 'Standing can be a specific activation condition', '**Classic orthostatic tremor has a high-frequency 13–18 Hz EMG pattern.**', ['The tremor can be difficult to see clinically.','Other standing-related movement syndromes require electrophysiological distinction.'], '**Confirm phenomenology rather than labeling all standing symptoms autonomic.**', ['Check blood pressure when indicated.','Assess associated neurological findings before calling it isolated.'], [['Standing still','Provokes tremor'],['Sitting/walking','Often relieves symptoms'],['Surface EMG','Confirms characteristic frequency']], [TR], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A hand tremor changes frequency to match rhythmic tapping by the opposite hand and diminishes with distraction. What is the best interpretation?', 'Positive signs support functional tremor when interpreted within a consistent examination',[
 ('These signs prove deliberate fabrication','Functional symptoms are not synonymous with conscious feigning.'),('A psychiatric history is required to diagnose it','Diagnosis rests on positive neurological signs rather than psychiatric history alone.'),('Any variability excludes functional tremor','Variability and entrainment can be supportive findings.')],
 'Use positive signs of altered motor control', '**Entrainment and distractibility can support a functional movement diagnosis.**', ['Demonstrate reproducible findings with respectful explanation.','An organic movement disorder can coexist.'], '**Offer a clear diagnosis and appropriate rehabilitation.**', ['Avoid communicating that symptoms are imaginary.','Assess additional neurological concerns on their own merits.'], [['Entrainment','Tremor changes toward tapping rhythm'],['Distractibility','Change with diverted attention'],['Meaning','Positive diagnostic signs, not proof of intent']], [TR], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which description best defines dystonia?', 'Sustained or intermittent muscle contractions causing patterned twisting movements or abnormal postures',[
 ('Random flowing movements that migrate unpredictably','This better describes chorea.'),('Brief shock-like jerks without a sustained patterned posture','This better describes myoclonus.'),('Rhythmic oscillation around a joint as the sole defining feature','That defines tremor rather than dystonia.')],
 'Describe the movement rather than its presumed cause', '**Patterning and directional posturing are characteristic of dystonia.**', ['Action can provoke or worsen the movement.','Overflow and a sensory trick can support recognition.'], '**Then classify distribution and etiology.**', ['Focal, segmental and generalized distributions have different implications.','Consider drug exposure and combined neurological syndromes.'], [['Dystonia','Patterned posture/twisting'],['Chorea','Irregular flowing movement'],['Myoclonus','Brief jerk']], [DY])
q('Painful involuntary neck rotation with consistent overactivity of selected cervical muscles persists despite simple measures. Which treatment commonly provides targeted symptomatic benefit?', 'Botulinum toxin injections into appropriately selected muscles',[
 ('Routine high-dose levodopa for all adult cervical dystonia','Levodopa response is important in selected syndromes, not a universal strategy.'),('Long-term haloperidol as the preferred focal treatment','It can cause additional movement disorders and is not the usual targeted choice.'),('Permanent complete neck immobilization','This does not provide an appropriate long-term treatment plan.')],
 'A focal syndrome permits focal treatment', '**Botulinum toxin reduces excessive muscle activation through local neuromuscular effects.**', ['Muscle selection and dose require expertise.','Dysphagia and local weakness are relevant adverse effects.'], '**Reassess the pattern at follow-up.**', ['Physiotherapy can complement injections.','Response and duration guide later treatment cycles.'], [['Target','Overactive muscles'],['Benefit','Posture and pain'],['Risk','Local weakness/dysphagia']], [DY], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Several hours after prochlorperazine, a young adult develops painful torticollis and an oculogyric crisis. What is the appropriate immediate approach?', 'Stop the offending drug and give prompt parenteral anticholinergic treatment, assessing airway involvement',[
 ('Continue prochlorperazine and await spontaneous progression','Continuing the cause can worsen the reaction.'),('Start a VMAT2 inhibitor as the immediate rescue treatment','This is not the usual rescue for acute dystonia.'),('Treat as chronic essential tremor with propranolol','The time course and posturing indicate acute drug-induced dystonia.')],
 'Acute dopamine blockade can produce a treatable emergency', '**Acute dystonia can involve ocular, cervical and laryngeal muscles.**', ['Use a locally appropriate parenteral anticholinergic such as benztropine or an anticholinergic antihistamine.','Laryngeal involvement requires urgent airway assessment.'], '**Prevent early recurrence after rescue.**', ['A short continuing regimen may be needed according to the drug duration and local protocol.','Document the reaction and review future antiemetic choices.'], [['Onset','Hours to days after exposure'],['Movement','Sustained painful posturing'],['Rescue','Anticholinergic and airway assessment']], [DY,TD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient on long-term dopamine receptor blockade develops persistent disabling oro-bucco-lingual stereotypies. Which pharmacological strategy has an established role?', 'A VMAT2 inhibitor such as valbenazine or deutetrabenazine after individualized assessment',[
 ('Escalate chronic benztropine as routine treatment of the dyskinesia','Anticholinergics do not reliably treat TD and may worsen it.'),('Abruptly discontinue all psychiatric treatment without coordination','Psychiatric relapse and withdrawal phenomena require coordinated decisions.'),('Treat solely with high-dose levodopa','This is not a standard TD strategy.')],
 'Tardive dyskinesia differs from acute dystonia', '**Persistent tardive hyperkinesia warrants structured assessment and treatment review.**', ['VMAT2 inhibition reduces presynaptic monoamine packaging.','Consider symptom severity, disability, interactions and product-specific precautions.'], '**Coordinate the dopamine-blocking regimen with psychiatric care.**', ['Stopping the causative drug may not promptly reverse TD.','Use serial movement ratings and functional goals.'], [['Acute dystonia','Often anticholinergic responsive'],['Tardive dyskinesia','VMAT2 treatment can help'],['Follow-up','Movement and psychiatric stability']], [TD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which genetic mechanism explains anticipation, especially with paternal transmission, in Huntington disease?', 'Expansion of a CAG repeat in HTT',[
 ('An FMR1 CGG premutation','This is relevant to fragile-X associated disorders, not HD.'),('An intronic GAA expansion in FXN','This causes Friedreich ataxia.'),('A CTG expansion in DMPK','This causes myotonic dystrophy type 1.')],
 'Repeat type and gene determine the disorder', '**HD is an autosomal dominant HTT repeat-expansion disorder.**', ['Intergenerational repeat expansion can shift onset earlier.','Paternal transmission is particularly associated with large expansions.'], '**Genetic testing requires counseling.**', ['Predictive testing differs from confirming a symptomatic diagnosis.','Discuss psychological, family and consent implications.'], [['HD','HTT, CAG'],['Friedreich ataxia','FXN, GAA'],['Myotonic dystrophy 1','DMPK, CTG']], [HD])
q('A patient with Huntington disease has functionally troublesome chorea and a history of major depression. What principle should guide VMAT2 inhibitor treatment?', 'Assess current mood, suicidality and product-specific contraindications before selecting and monitoring treatment',[
 ('Treat the movement score without assessing mood','The psychiatric context can materially alter treatment safety.'),('Use a VMAT2 inhibitor to prevent all cognitive progression','These are symptomatic treatments, not established cures.'),('Assume every choreic movement requires maximum-dose medication','Treat disability and balance adverse effects rather than normalizing every visible movement.')],
 'Chorea treatment must account for the whole HD phenotype', '**VMAT2 inhibitors can reduce chorea but require attention to psychiatric risk.**', ['Depression and suicidal risk are important in HD itself.','Specific warnings and contraindications differ by product and indication.'], '**Define the target functional problem.**', ['Monitor sedation, parkinsonism and swallowing.','Behavior, nutrition, cognition and family support also need care.'], [['Target','Troublesome chorea'],['Safety','Mood and suicidality review'],['Expectation','Symptomatic benefit']], [HD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Acute large-amplitude flinging movements affect the left arm and leg after a small deep infarct. Which circuit localization is classically implicated?', 'The right subthalamic region and connected basal-ganglia motor network',[
 ('The left subthalamic region alone','A supratentorial basal-ganglia lesion classically causes contralateral movements.'),('An isolated left dorsal root lesion','This cannot explain a hemibody ballistic hyperkinesia.'),('The right neuromuscular junction','NMJ disease causes fatigable weakness rather than ballism.')],
 'Ballism is a circuit syndrome', '**Contralateral subthalamic dysfunction is a classic association with hemiballism.**', ['Lesions in other connected structures can produce the syndrome.','Acute onset warrants stroke and metabolic assessment.'], '**Avoid restricting causality to one nucleus.**', ['Review imaging and glucose.','Treat the cause and consider symptomatic therapy if injury or severe disability results.'], [['Ballism','Proximal flinging hyperkinesia'],['Classic lesion','Contralateral subthalamic region'],['Other causes','Connected network or metabolic disturbance']], [HD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Brief interruptions of sustained wrist extension produce irregular flapping in a patient with metabolic encephalopathy. How is this movement classified?', 'Negative myoclonus, clinically recognized as asterixis',[
 ('Positive myoclonus from a brief muscle contraction','Asterixis reflects a lapse in sustained activation.'),('A regular essential action tremor','The lapses are irregular rather than a stable rhythmic oscillation.'),('Dystonia from sustained agonist-antagonist contraction','This is not the mechanism of the brief postural lapses.')],
 'A jerk can result from contraction or interrupted contraction', '**Negative myoclonus is a brief pause in ongoing muscle activity.**', ['Asterixis is a clinical example.','Positive myoclonus is produced by a brief contraction.'], '**Look for the underlying cause rather than assuming hepatic disease.**', ['Uremia, respiratory failure and drugs can also cause asterixis.','Focal findings warrant consideration of a structural lesion.'], [['Positive myoclonus','Brief contraction'],['Negative myoclonus','Brief loss of activity'],['Asterixis','Postural negative myoclonus']], [MY])

